WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.120
CREB is switched on by adding a phosphate.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.460
NFAT is switched on by taking phosphates off.

3
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.400
In a resting T cell, NFAT stays in the cytoplasm.

4
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.360
Its many phosphates hide its signal for import into the nucleus.

5
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:29.353
When the T-cell receptor fires, calcium rises and stays high.

6
00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:35.053
Calcium and calmodulin switch on calcineurin, a protein phosphatase.

7
00:00:35.993 --> 00:00:38.753
Calcineurin strips the phosphates from NFAT.

8
00:00:39.613 --> 00:00:43.773
The import signal is exposed, and NFAT enters the nucleus.

9
00:00:44.593 --> 00:00:50.393
There it binds DNA beside a Fos-Jun pair, on genes such as interleukin-2.

10
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:57.007
Cyclosporin A and FK506 both shut T cells down.

11
00:00:57.787 --> 00:01:00.127
Each is held by a small helper protein.

12
00:01:01.147 --> 00:01:07.667
FK506 is held by FKBP12, and cyclosporin A by cyclophilin.

13
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:14.387
In 1991, Schreiber's group and collaborators reported that each drug, held by its

14
00:01:14.387 --> 00:01:17.167
helper, binds and inhibits calcineurin.

15
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:19.547
But that was a test tube result.

16
00:01:19.547 --> 00:01:21.947
Is that why the T cell shuts down?

17
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:28.740
In 1992, Neil Clipstone and Gerald Crabtree answered it.

18
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.220
They made T cells produce extra calcineurin.

19
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.980
Those cells were more resistant to both drugs, and NFAT-driven transcription went

20
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:44.860
up. If more enzyme means more drug is needed, the enzyme is the target.

21
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.260
Jam calcineurin, and NFAT keeps its phosphates and stays in the cytoplasm.

22
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.400
So the interleukin-2 gene stays quiet.
